= Evidence — Simple =

'''Evidence''' is information or material that gives us a reason to change how strongly we accept or reject a claim.

Examples include:

* an observation;
* a measurement;
* an experiment;
* a document;
* a recording;
* testimony;
* a repeated result;
* a physical trace.

Evidence is not the same as truth. A piece of evidence can be weak, misleading, incomplete, or later shown to be unreliable.

Evidence also has to be connected to a question. A pile of data is not automatically useful evidence unless we know what it measures and how it relates to the claim.

Different kinds of claims need different kinds of evidence. A mathematical claim may need a proof. A historical claim may need documents. A medical claim may need experiments and observations.

[[Empirical evidence/Simple|Empirical evidence]] comes from observation, experiment, or measurement.

Evidence is closely related to [[Justification/Simple|justification]] and [[Knowledge/Simple|knowledge]].

